Z||000015||theogre||12-28-2000||07:43 PM||||Building an oven isn't a bad idea. Just make sure the heater doesn't pose an ignition source in your spray area. The loose coating powder can be flamable, even explosive, under the right conditions.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Phil||12-28-2000||08:15 PM||||I see that the IR lamp only does 10 square inches at a time. Seems that it could take quite awhile to do most pieces.||||reg||1
Z||000017||william_thorniii||12-28-2000||08:48 PM||||Phil,<P>That is precisely why I am going with a MUCH larger IR lamp.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000018||Slick781||12-28-2000||10:31 PM||||could someone explain to me exactly what powder coating is?<P>David||||reg||1
Z||000019||william_thorniii||12-28-2000||10:50 PM||||slick,<P>Powder coating is essentially painting; however, one shoots electrically charged powder at an electrically charged metal part.  This process significantly reduces overspray since the powder is attracted to the surface.  Furthermore cleaning up is made much easier.  No more laying down all the paper making sure you don't spray the floor... it just sweeps up.  After shooting the powder, it must be cured with heat.  Other advantages or powder coating are called out earlier in this thread (primarily the durability of the finish).  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
